{"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/functionalgraphs\/functionalgraph\/cycledecomposition()"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph\/cycleDecomposition()"},"sections":[],"metadata":{"role":"symbol","modules":[{"name":"FunctionalGraphs"}],"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"func"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"cycleDecomposition"},{"kind":"text","text":"() -> "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"FunctionalGraph","preciseIdentifier":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV"},{"kind":"text","text":"."},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"CycleDecomposition","preciseIdentifier":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV18CycleDecompositionV"}],"remoteSource":{"fileName":"CycleDecomposition.swift","url":"https:\/\/github.com\/Tyler-Keith-Thompson\/Grafluent\/blob\/main\/Sources\/FunctionalGraphs\/CycleDecomposition.swift#L269"},"symbolKind":"method","extendedModule":"FunctionalGraphs","externalID":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV18cycleDecompositionAC05CycleE0VyF","title":"cycleDecomposition()","roleHeading":"Instance Method"},"kind":"symbol","primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"platforms":["Linux"],"languages":["swift"],"tokens":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"cycleDecomposition","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"() -> ","kind":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph","text":"FunctionalGraph","preciseIdentifier":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph\/CycleDecomposition","text":"CycleDecomposition","preciseIdentifier":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV18CycleDecompositionV","kind":"typeIdentifier"}]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"kind":"content","content":[{"level":2,"text":"Discussion","anchor":"discussion","type":"heading"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A counting sort of f gives the preimage rows and the in-degrees. Peeling the vertices of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"in-degree zero, repeatedly, visits the tree vertices leaves first, summing subtree sizes on"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the way, and leaves exactly the cyclic ones; each cycle is then walked from its least"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"vertex. Revisiting the peeled vertices in reverse, parents first, gives every tail length"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"and root and a preorder numbering of the trees from the subtree sizes; a counting sort by"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"depth then lays out the level-ancestor table. Nothing recurses or chases a chain of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"dependent reads, so a tail of 10⁷ vertices needs no call stack."}]},{"style":"note","type":"aside","name":"Precondition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The graph has fewer than 2³¹ vertices.","type":"text"}]}]}]}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The cycles of the graph, and every vertex’s tail, root and preimages. O(n) time, with no"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"allocation per vertex. The result holds 5n + 3 "},{"code":"Int","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"s (two "},{"code":"Int","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" arrays for the preimage"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"rows, one for the vertices by depth, a 12-byte record per vertex and a 4-byte preorder"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"number beside each vertex by depth), plus one per cycle and one per unit of the longest"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"tail; building it takes 3n more for a moment (two 12-byte entries per vertex)."}],"schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"minor":3,"major":0},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs","doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph"]]},"references":{"doc://FunctionalGraphs/documentation/FunctionalGraphs/FunctionalGraph":{"role":"symbol","title":"FunctionalGraph","url":"\/documentation\/functionalgraphs\/functionalgraph","type":"topic","kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"FunctionalGraph","kind":"identifier"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"FunctionalGraph","kind":"identifier"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph","abstract":[{"text":"The graph of a function f on the vertices ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0..<n"},{"text":": every vertex ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"v"},{"text":" has exactly one out-edge,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"v → f(v)"},{"text":", and a fixed point is a self-loop. Also called a successor graph (Laaksonen,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Competitive Programmer’s Handbook"}]},{"text":", §16.3).","type":"text"}]},"doc://FunctionalGraphs/documentation/FunctionalGraphs":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs","title":"FunctionalGraphs","abstract":[],"kind":"symbol","role":"collection","url":"\/documentation\/functionalgraphs","type":"topic"},"doc://FunctionalGraphs/documentation/FunctionalGraphs/FunctionalGraph/CycleDecomposition":{"title":"FunctionalGraph.CycleDecomposition","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"CycleDecomposition","kind":"identifier"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"CycleDecomposition","kind":"identifier"}],"kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/functionalgraphs\/functionalgraph\/cycledecomposition","type":"topic","role":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"Every cycle of a functional graph, and for each vertex its tail and the cycle it reaches, in","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"Flajolet and Odlyzko’s terms: walking from ","type":"text"},{"code":"v","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", the first vertex on a cycle is ","type":"text"},{"code":"root(of: v)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":",","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"tailLength(of: v)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" steps away (Sage’s “preperiod”), on a cycle of ","type":"text"},{"code":"cycleLength(of: v)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"vertices. For a permutation, ","type":"text"},{"code":"cycles","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is its cycle decomposition and every tail is zero.","type":"text"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph\/CycleDecomposition"},"doc://FunctionalGraphs/documentation/FunctionalGraphs/FunctionalGraph/cycleDecomposition()":{"role":"symbol","title":"cycleDecomposition()","url":"\/documentation\/functionalgraphs\/functionalgraph\/cycledecomposition()","type":"topic","kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"cycleDecomposition","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"() -> ","kind":"text"},{"text":"FunctionalGraph","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"text":"CycleDecomposition","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV18CycleDecompositionV"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph\/cycleDecomposition()","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The cycles of the graph, and every vertex’s tail, root and preimages. O(n) time, with no"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"allocation per vertex. The result holds 5n + 3 "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Int"},{"type":"text","text":"s (two "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Int"},{"type":"text","text":" arrays for the preimage"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"rows, one for the vertices by depth, a 12-byte record per vertex and a 4-byte preorder"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"number beside each vertex by depth), plus one per cycle and one per unit of the longest"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"tail; building it takes 3n more for a moment (two 12-byte entries per vertex)."}]}}}